    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, a classic case of the \"invisible barrier\" or the \"sheet tab that looks right but acts like a mischievous goblin.\" Sometimes, even if the sheet name is spelled perfectly, Excel might be hiding a tiny, invisible sheet named \"Sheet1\" right next to it, or perhaps the sheet is currently hidden, which acts like a goblin hiding in the shadows, preventing the macro from seeing the real destination.\n\nSince the sheet exists but the copy fails, try using the `Range.Copy` method with a specific destination range object rather than relying on the sheet name directly. You can also explicitly check if the sheet is visible. Here is a revised approach that handles the \"goblin\" (hidden sheet) issue and uses a more robust copy method:\n\n```vba\nSub MonthlyRecon()\n    Dim wsSource As Worksheet\n    Dim wsDest As Worksheet\n    Dim destRange As Range\n    \n    Set wsSource = ThisWorkbook.Sheets(\"Data_Input\")\n    \n    ' Check if the destination sheet exists and is visible\n    On Error Resume Next\n    Set wsDest = ThisWorkbook.Sheets(\"Recon_Report\")\n    If wsDest Is Nothing Then\n        Set wsDest = This",
